00:47:02.403 --> 00:48:24.683
<v SPEAKER_3>If that is indeed the case, I see no reason why the patent should not be issued. Now, granted, the US Patent Office can act somewhat strangely sometimes. Just because a patent should be valid doesn't necessarily mean it will be issued, but most likely it would be issued. In which case Microsoft could not use this technology without first obtaining a patent license from me. And I of course would only license this patent for socially responsible purposes. On the other hand, Microsoft may upon searching the files further determine that, oh, they've actually thought of this long before I did. Well, if so, then Microsoft will need to provide documentation of this fact to the Patent Office to prevent the Patent Office from either issuing the patent or more likely, this is how these things are done in patent law, or more likely challenge my patent as it has been issued claiming prior art. If so, there will be an extensive discovery period during which myself and my attorneys will get to examine Microsoft's internal documents on Palladium and PCPA, which undoubtedly will include the intended uses of the technology. So no matter what the outcome is, I believe the consumer wins.

00:48:25.863 --> 00:48:27.943
<v SPEAKER_5>Ja, es gibt im Grunde zwei Szenarios.

