00:57:24.623 --> 00:57:42.623
<v SPEAKER_7>So Lucky, do you think that the current wave of that more and more hardware manufacturers sign on to TCPA that it will continue or do you think that there might be someone recognizing, saying okay, there might be a market niche for me producing non-TCPA hardware maybe in Taiwan?

00:57:44.943 --> 00:58:57.383
<v SPEAKER_3>In the PC space, I believe it is extremely unlikely that non-TCPA, non-Palladium hardware will survive for the same reason that AMD signed on to the initiative. Initially, when Microsoft approached Intel, telling Intel, listen folks, we need particular features for the CPU, we need a new Ring-1 functionality in the CPU. Intel was very happy because they thought along these lines before with processor ID and encrypted instruction sets and so forth. In Microsoft, after Intel agreed and went to AMD and said, told AMD, listen folks, you have two choices. You can either provide these features in your CPU or Windows 2005 will not run on AMD chips. Which of the two is it going to be? Well, I think AMD's answer was pretty much a given. And that's in fact the answer that they gave. I don't believe that there is a hardware manufacturer, be that in the US or in Taiwan, that can economically produce current generation CPUs without the Windows market. Without being the CPUs being used for the Windows market.

00:59:12.323 --> 00:59:18.403
